# Cursor Rules — English Learning System

This file is the project's constitution. Every slice reads it first.

## Before writing any code

1. Read `docs/PRD.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `docs/TASKS.md`.
2. Read `BUILD_PROGRESS.md` (repo root) to see what already exists.
3. Identify the single slice you are being asked to build. Read its spec in
   `specs/` and its acceptance criteria. If that spec file does not exist,
   stop and ask — do not improvise.
4. If the slice is ambiguous or conflicts with the spec, STOP and ask.

## Scope

- One slice per prompt. Implement the slice's spec file in `specs/` and
  nothing else.
- Never begin a slice before the previous one meets its acceptance criteria.
- No dependency added unless the current slice needs it; add it to
  `requirements.txt` in the same change.
- No handler, service or prompt file created ahead of the slice that
  implements it.
- Do not "improve" code outside the current slice, even if it looks wrong.
  Note it in Known Issues instead.

## Architecture (non-negotiable)

- Plain SQL only. No ORM, no Alembic. Migrations are numbered `.sql` files in
  `migrations/`, tracked in `schema_version`.
- PostgreSQL via `psycopg[binary,pool]` against the Supabase **session-mode**
  pooler. Never `supabase-py`.
- Every LLM, STT and TTS call goes through `app/llm.py` or `app/speech.py`.
  A provider SDK is imported nowhere else.
- Every user-facing string lives in `app/texts.py`. Never inline a message in
  a handler or service.
- Every environment variable is read in `app/config.py` and nowhere else.
- All prompts live in `app/prompts/*.txt` and are loaded at runtime.
- All queries scoped by `user_id`. No query may ever return another user's rows.
- Excluded by decision: ORM, Redis, Celery, Docker, web framework.

## Behaviour

- Fail loud to the operator, soft to the user. A user never sees a stack
  trace or an exception message.
- Copy follows `docs/PRD.md` §8: under 400 characters for scheduled messages,
  one idea per message, never guilt, buttons over typing.
- Telegram truncates inline button labels to the button width. Never put content
  the user must READ inside a button label. Buttons carry short labels only
  (numbers, single words, ≤20 chars). Anything longer goes in the message body.
  This applies to every current and future question format.
- Audio is never written to disk.
- Max 2 nudges per user per day. Max 3 bot-initiated messages per user per day.
- Tasks stay open until 03:00 the following night. Never expire content.
- Rescue mode never presents a backlog.
- Weekly success is measured against 5 days out of 7, never 7.

## Code standards

- Python 3.12, type hints on all function signatures.
- No secrets in code. Everything through `config.Settings`.
- Functions do one thing. If a handler exceeds ~40 lines, extract to `services/`.

## Testing

- After building, state exactly how to verify the slice manually in Telegram,
  step by step.
- Automated tests are required only for `services/errors.py` (spacing ladder)
  and `services/streaks.py` (freeze, rescue). Write them when those slices
  land — a silent bug in either invalidates the product without being visible.

## Every slice ends by

- Updating `BUILD_PROGRESS.md`: slice row, header fields, file inventory,
  decisions log, known issues, next action.
- Marking the slice 🟡 code-complete. Only the human marks ✅, after verifying
  in Telegram.
- Outputting a short summary: what was built, what to test, what is next.
  Do not start the next slice.
